WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:04.753
- [Narrator] "Star Trek" is an
iconic series from the 1960s.

00:00:04.753 --> 00:00:07.460
It's also one of the most
culturally progressive shows

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.070
of its time.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.330
This is due, in large part, to a strong,

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:14.380
unforgettable female officer
onboard the Starship.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.770
- My name is Nichelle Nichols,
and I played Lieutenant Uhura

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.426
on "Star Trek," and I did a damn good job.

00:00:22.426 --> 00:00:25.093
(upbeat music)

00:00:27.595 --> 00:00:29.510
- [Narrator] The 1960s were the height

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:31.800
of the American Civil Rights movement.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.440
At the very same time, Nichelle
Nichols was one of the only

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.470
black women on TV who played a confident

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.590
and strong character.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.410
- I want immortality.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.630
- [Narrator] But had it not
been for a certain super fan

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.100
at an NAACP dinner,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.100
Nichelle would've never
made history like she did.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.170
- He came over.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:50.210
He was really excited, and I looked at him

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.002
and it was Martin Luther King.

00:00:52.002 --> 00:00:54.000
- [Narrator] She told him
she was leaving Star Trek

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.760
only after taping season one.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.930
- And he stopped and he said,

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.420
you can't leave.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.810
You don't have a black role.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:03.570
You have an equal role.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.489
And I realized I had a
responsibility to that character,

00:01:08.489 --> 00:01:11.134
to all those people out there.

00:01:11.134 --> 00:01:14.853
And that responsibility
becomes part of your life.

00:01:18.091 --> 00:01:20.070
- [Narrator] Nichelle
would further make history

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.030
performing one of television's first

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.130
interracial kissing scenes.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.690
In the episode, Captain Kirk
played by William Shatner

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.390
is forced by aliens to kiss Uhura.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.890
When NBC executives learned of the kiss

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.260
they became concerned it
would anger TV stations

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:38.400
in the deep south.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.902
- The director said we'll do
it both ways with the kiss

00:01:41.902 --> 00:01:43.600
and without the kiss.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.010
- [Narrator] So William Shatner
came up with a master plan.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.240
During filming, he botched
the tapes without the kiss

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.360
on purpose leaving the kissing
scene as the only good option

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.490
to use on TV.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:57.130
- To a young black woman in
a situation that has never

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.973
been done on television, I
realized how important it was.

00:02:05.536 --> 00:02:06.370
(upbeat music)

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:08.614
- [Narrator] Years later when the original

00:02:08.614 --> 00:02:10.240
"Star Trek" series ended,
Nichelle was hired by NASA

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.650
to make their space
program a bit more diverse.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:16.890
- I recruited people
like me to go into space.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.963
Whether they were black,
white, yellow, brown, red.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.350
People like Sally Ride, Guion Bluford.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:29.350
I also recruited Mae Jemison,
the first black woman

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:31.552
to go into space.

00:02:31.552 --> 00:02:33.730
- [Narrator] Nichelle Nichols'
time on "Star Trek" was

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:37.350
instrumental in advancing the
image of African Americans,

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.170
and at a time when such
equality was unheard of;

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.200
and for that, her legacy shall ...

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.320
- Live long and prosper.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.503
Live long and prosper, baby.

